## 3. Mutation verification

Each row below breaks one specific thing in `preview.rs`, runs the integration test
that is supposed to care about it, and restores the file. A row that still passes is
reported as VACUOUS rather than quietly dropped from the tally.

| # | mutation | outcome |
|---|---|---|
| M1 | outer `Host` forwarded instead of the app's own | CAUGHT |
| M2 | `Set-Cookie` `Path` left unscoped | CAUGHT |
| M3 | loopback-only guard disabled | CAUGHT (two separate tests) |
| M4 | `Upgrade` removed from the hop-by-hop list | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M5 | `Location` left unprefixed | CAUGHT |
| M6 | browser's `Sec-WebSocket-Key` reused upstream | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M7 | host bearer token forwarded upstream | CAUGHT |
| M8 | the client's own five headers appended rather than inserted | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M9 | upstream `Upgrade` left alongside the browser's copy | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M10 | upstream `Host` left alongside the browser's copy | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M11 | `Connection` neither dropped nor replaced | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M12 | browser's `Host` neither dropped nor replaced | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |
| M13 | rewritten `Host` not pushed at all | VACUOUS (equivalent mutant) |

Five real defects are caught. The eight equivalent mutants have two separate causes,
and both were only found by running the mutations -- the code comments had asserted a
third cause that turned out to be false.

**M4, M8, M9, M10, M11, M12, M13 -- one cause.** `tungstenite`'s
`handshake::client::generate_request` writes `Host`, `Connection`, `Upgrade`,
`Sec-WebSocket-Version` and `Sec-WebSocket-Key` itself, and for each name it calls
`HeaderMap::remove`, which returns the first value and removes *every* value under that
name. Extra copies are therefore dropped silently, and the `Error::Protocol(
InvalidHeader)` branch that looks for a surviving second copy is unreachable through a
`HeaderMap`. The earlier comment in `bridge_websocket` claimed the client "rejects a
request that carries a second copy", which is what the `for (k, v) in headers` loop in
that function appears to say and is not what happens in practice. It has been corrected
to describe the first-value-wins behavior.

M12 and M13 have one further reason: `into_client_request()` seeds the header map with
the authority from the URI, and the bridge builds that URI from the same `target.port`
the rewrite uses, so the two ways of producing `Host` always agree and no integration
test can tell them apart.

Both facts are now pinned by a unit test rather than asserted in prose.
`the_websocket_client_puts_one_of_its_own_headers_on_the_wire` calls `generate_request`
directly, appends a second value under each of the five names, and makes the URI (port
3000) disagree with the header map (port 9999). The assertion that goes on the wire is
`host: 127.0.0.1:9999` -- the map wins, which is what makes the rewrite the value the
app reads -- and no `a-second-copy` appears anywhere. If a future `tungstenite` joins
values instead of taking the first, or prefers the URI over the map, that test fails in
place of this paragraph.

**M6 -- a different cause.** Each hop validates `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` against the key
it actually sent, and the raw-socket stand-in derives its answer from the received key,
so reusing the browser's key upstream is not observable as a failure. It is also
harmless: both hops would compute the same accept value. Recorded as an equivalent
mutant rather than a coverage gap.

