Reading of the parts that matter:

- Line 1: the host says at startup what it will preview and over which reach
  (`loopback only`), so the enabled set is not something you have to probe for.
- Line 2: `set-cookie: sid=abc; Path=/preview/39757/; HttpOnly` -- the app asked for
  `Path=/`; the rewrite scoped it to the preview and dropped `Domain`, so the app
  cannot set a cookie the `/api/*` routes would receive.
- Line 4: the app's `Location: /main.js` became `/preview/39757/main.js`. Left alone it
  would have pointed at this host's root, i.e. at a route that is not the app's.
- Lines 5-7: `origin_not_allowed`, `preview_port_not_allowed` (naming what *is*
  enabled, which is deliberate -- a bare 403 here sends people to the config file),
  `preview_upstream_unreachable`, and `preview_loopback_only` distinguished from each
  other rather than collapsed into one 500.
- Line 7/8: the same host by its declared public name refuses previews until
  `CHAOS_WEB_PREVIEW_ALLOW_PUBLIC=1` is set. Forging `Host: 127.0.0.1.evil.example` is
  not loopback either; that is a unit test and an integration test of its own
  (`a_forged_loopback_hostname_is_not_loopback`).
- Line 9: what the dev server recorded. `host` and `origin` are its own authority, not
  the host's; `authorization`, `referer` and `cookie` are all empty in both directions;
  `x-forwarded-prefix` carries the prefix so a server-rendered app can build its URLs
  under it.
- Line 10: `/health` still 200, `/api/sessions` still 401 without a credential, and the
  API's 64 KiB body cap still returns 413 at 70 KiB while a 200 KiB POST through the
  preview succeeds -- the preview path does not inherit the API's request-body limit,
  and the API path does not lose its own.

## What this does not cover

- **No real browser.** There is no browser automation in this environment, so cookie
  scoping is checked on the `Set-Cookie` value the proxy returns, not by a browser jar
  deciding what to send back to `/api/*`. The rewrite is the part under test; the
  browser honoring `Path=/preview/3000/` is RFC 6265 behavior this repo does not
  implement.
- **No real Vite.** The stand-in is a Python/axum server configured with Vite's
  strictness (`Host`/`Origin` checked, one named subprotocol offered). A real Vite HMR
  client decides for itself which host and port its socket connects to; a dev server
  that hard-codes its own port produces a socket that bypasses this host. That is the
  app's code, not the proxy's, and it is written down as a limitation in the module
  docs and in `CONTRIBUTING.md` rather than claimed as working. Prefix proxying needs
  `base: '/preview/3000/'` on the app side.
- **`permessage-deflate` is not bridged.** `Sec-WebSocket-Extensions` is dropped on the
  upstream hop, so the upstream connection is uncompressed. Frames still cross the
  bridge correctly (the bridge moves decoded messages, not bytes); nothing negotiates
  compression with the dev server.
- **No cross-site test with a real second origin in a browser.** The `Origin` refusal is
  exercised over HTTP with a hand-set header, which is the actual code path -- the
  browser would set the same header.
- **The bridge is message-level, so a mid-frame TCP reset on one hop is not propagated
  as a close code on the other.** Both hops see an error; the specific code may not
  survive. Not exercised.

## How the walkthrough was run

The script and the stand-in dev server are not committed (they duplicate what the
integration tests drive with more moving parts); the transcript above is their output.
In outline: build `chaos-web`, start `fake_dev_server.py` on a port chosen at run time
(it refuses a `Host`/`Origin` that is not its own and appends one JSON line per request
to a log), start `chaos-web` bound to `127.0.0.1:0` with `CHAOS_WEB_TOKEN`,
`CHAOS_WEB_PREVIEW_PORTS` set to the dev server's port plus one dead port, and
`CHAOS_WEB_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=chaos.example.test:8443`, then issue the requests above with
`curl --resolve` for the public-name cases, restart with
`CHAOS_WEB_PREVIEW_ALLOW_PUBLIC=1` for case 8, and print the dev server's log at the
end.
